WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
Run, price and prove a small security audit.

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.800
This is the video for module 3 of Cybersecurity Fundamentals.

3
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:15.730
When you review someone else's security, the first document is not a technical report. It is written permission.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.740
Then you find the real gaps, mostly by asking questions and looking at settings with the owner.

5
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:30.540
You price the work clearly, and you prove what changed, in numbers. That is how trust, and paid work, grows.

6
00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:41.070
Step one is a signed authorisation from someone with authority. It says what you may look at, which systems, which dates, and what you must not do.

7
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:54.180
Nigeria's Cybercrimes Act 2015 makes unauthorised access an offence. So never log in, scan or send test phishing without clear written permission for exactly that.

8
00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:06.410
Next, interview the leader, then staff and admins. Ask who handles payments, who the admins are, which accounts have two-step verification, and what has gone wrong before.

9
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.680
Then check settings together. The owner clicks, you watch and take notes. Passwords stay with their owner.

10
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:20.480
Finally, score each finding with likelihood times impact, as you learned in module 1.

11
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:36.530
For example, Zainab reviews a private primary school in Ibadan with 14 staff email accounts. With the admin officer beside her, she counts 11 accounts with 2-Step Verification. That is about 79%.

12
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.310
There are 4 super admins, including a teacher who left last term. The target is 2.

13
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:53.070
The pupils' records spreadsheet is shared with anyone who has the link. That scores 4 times 5, which is 20, her top finding.

14
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.550
And the bursar accepts bank changes by email with no call-back. That scores 15.

15
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:08.070
Each finding is written as a fact, with why it matters, its score, and a fix with an owner and date. No fear language, no blame.

16
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:19.190
Now pricing. Zainab breaks the work into phases: discovery 6 hours, hardening 10, training 4, and report 3. That is 23 hours.

17
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:26.740
At an illustrative rate of 8,000 naira an hour, the base fee is 184,000 naira.

18
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:33.090
She adds 15% for unknowns, which is 27,600 naira.

19
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:45.960
That is 211,600 naira, which she rounds to 212,000. She asks for 50% on signing and 50% when she delivers the report.

20
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.690
Tools the school buys, like a backup drive, are listed separately, not hidden in her fee.

21
00:02:52.940 --> 00:03:02.800
Write both lists. In scope: the 14 staff accounts, the Facebook page settings, backups with one restore test, and the report and incident plan.

22
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:16.480
Out of scope: the bank portal, website testing, and monitoring after handover. And no honest professional can promise that no attack will ever succeed. Promise checkable actions instead.

23
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:23.360
If the client asks for extra work midway, write it down, quote it, and get approval in writing first.

24
00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:36.690
For example, Ngozi leads security for a 20-person NGO in Port Harcourt. Before training, 9 of 20 staff had 2-Step Verification. That is 45%.

25
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:51.160
In her session she teaches three behaviours only: check the real sender, call back on numbers on file, and report anything odd at once. Everyone turns on 2-Step together, and reporting is blame-free.

26
00:03:52.410 --> 00:04:01.540
A week later, all 20 are enrolled. Next month, reports went from 1 to 7. That is good news: people are noticing and speaking up.

27
00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:11.840
Turn your work into case studies: the situation, what you did, results in numbers, anonymised documents, and a short reference with permission.

28
00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:20.060
Never show passwords, recovery codes, staff names or personal data. Trust is the real product of security work.

29
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:38.630
To remember. Get written authorisation before any review. Write findings as facts with a score, fix, owner and date. Your fee is hours times rate, plus a stated contingency. Write both scope lists. And measure before and after, so you can show what changed.

30
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:50.250
Your tasks: run a mini discovery with signed permission, draft your audit proposal with the fee shown, and deliver an awareness session with before and after numbers.

31
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.540
Continue with the lessons below.
